From: Onigiri
Subject: [PATCH] Rework Software update into its own page with notifications

The Software update row in Onigiri Software opens a page (UpdateSettingsFragment,
res/xml/onigiri_update_settings.xml): the release on offer with its size, what's new since the
installed build, Check for updates and Notify me about updates, and a bar pinned under the list
with Download and install, which asks first and then shows the download's progress and Cancel,
or that it failed. Builds too old for an update get the release page instead. Install from
storage picks a package file, copies it into Settings' files (checking it is an OTA zip) and asks
like Download and install, saying when it isn't the package listed for this build. The header and
What's new are TextPreferences: AndroidX greys the title of a row that can't be tapped. The bar
takes One UI's blue from the app theme's colorPrimary (android:colorAccent is teal here).

Updates reads update.json from the latest release (scripts/ota_manifest.py); onigiri-update.sh
still downloads and installs as root, given the tag and sha256 in sys.onigiri.update.*.
UpdateJobService checks twice a day and posts one notification per release; UpdateReceiver
schedules it at boot and says so after an update installed. The classes get their own dex
(smali_classes5): smali_classes4 is at the 64K method reference limit. The smali is built from
onigiri/mods/settings/java/update (its build.sh: Java against SDK 35 and stubs of Settings'
classes, d8, baksmali); change the Java there and rebuild rather than editing the smali.

